sues and promoting overall well-being. The organization also collaborates with various entities to ensure that these guidelines are effectively implemented and adapted to local contexts [Data: Relationships (714, 5923, 5945, 5955, 5938, +more)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Collaboration with Countries for Data Collection\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Countries like Brazil, Ghana, and India play significant roles in providing data on child growth and nutrition to the WHO. This collaboration is essential for establishing international growth reference patterns and nutritional standards. The data collected from these countries helps the WHO to develop comprehensive insights into global health trends and challenges, thereby supporting targeted health initiatives. The involvement of these countries highlights the importance of local data in shaping global health policies [Data: Entities (1306, 15271, 15270); Relationships (5962, 5964, 5963, 5968, 4473, +more)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Impact of CIE-11 on Disease Classification\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The CIE-11 (International Classification of Diseases, 11th Revision) is a significant tool developed by the WHO for classifying diseases and health conditions. This classification system includes parameters for evaluating nutritional status, which is crucial for understanding health issues beyond just diseases. The CIE-11's implementation aids in standardizing health data collection and reporting, facilitating better health management and research globally. Its role in disease classification is vital for public health surveillance and intervention strategies [Data: Entities (5459); Relationships (5935, 5924, 5925, 14921, 5940, +more)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Research on Obesity and Health Issues\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Organizations like Di@bet.es and RICA are involved in researching obesity and its related health i